after two years of wrangling the budget  battles have come to an end with an  agreement for the eu's long-term budget  up to 2020 but some wouldn't let it go  without a fight  and those of course who call for new  owned resources are the same people who  consistently call for more europe as if  throwing money at the problem will  somehow solve it in total the eu will  have up to 960 billion euros to spend  over the next seven years it represents  a 3.5 percent decrease from the last  budget and the first reduction in the  eu's history helga trooper dubbed it a  budget of missed chances saying the  priorities for wide of the mark and size  matters it's not our  selfish interest the eu budget because  it's for the european citizens and  therefore it would have been the better  way to spend  more with other and more future oriented  priorities it was an opinion not shared  by all the parliament  that in fact  talked  about  the eu has agreed to spend less but more  wisely the common agricultural policy  will get the largest slice of the pie  with 380 billion euros regional policy  took the largest hit but still receives  325 billion euros the horizon 2020  program will have over 70 billion euros  at its disposal and the eu is hoping its  erasmus plus scheme will boost  employability for the young in exchange  for the budget cuts mep secured some  compromises a midterm review in 2016  meaning that if economic circumstances  change as the politicians may following  the european elections next year there  is room for maneuver they will also have  a working group to look into how the  budget can become self-sufficient  through its own resources and made sure  next year's payments will be met the man  who steered the report through  parliament feels it was the least worst  deal the alternative of this budget is  no budget it's not perfect again but  this is much better than live with  uncertainty now that the seven-year  budget has passed meps can get to work  on how best to spend it starting with a  vote on wednesday on next year's budget
 

